  <participating-org role="4" type="11">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reseaux de la jeunesse nigerienne s</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Niger Youth Network on Climate Change (RJNCC) is the national representation of African Youth Initiative on Climate Change (AYICC) which is the largest platform for African youth on environmental and climate issues with the African Union and its branches
The RJNCC is a group of youth organizations working in the fields of the environment, climate change and renewable energy for Sustainable Development.
the Niger Youth Network on Climate Change (RJNCC.AYICC-Niger) is a shared research and training organization which brings together leaders of youth organizations from the Republic of Niger. It essentially establishes interactive links between the various partner structures in order to encourage the dissemination and exchange of information as well asthe development of policies and programs concerning youth, climate change, the environment and sustainable development. . Such a networking space has many advantages such as the synergy of resources, the improvement of the quality of service, informationand scientific communications, better sharing of knowledge and experiences.
However, it also poses many questions: What type of organization between partners to set up? What information to disseminate? Which technical supports to choose to communicate? What new uses, what new practices does it bring about? What methods and strategies adopted for the defense and popularization of conventions, treaties and other legislative texts in the field of climate change, the environment and sustainable development?What social and economic reintegration policies to put in place as part of green youth employment?
The Niger Youth Network on Climate Change (RJNCC.AYICC-Niger) is a non-governmental, non-profit, apolitical and non-denominational youth information and training organization on youth issues. Its creation, which took place on July 10, 2010, is based on the observation that young people are constantly asked to take part in national development. Hence the need for these young people to be informed and trained to contribute to the best of their abilities. However, the reality today is that young people often ignore important problems for which they could provide solutions. It also appears that the unemployment problem is crucial and is partly due to the lack of professionalism in education on the one hand and the lack of private initiative on the other.</narrative>
